---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import commits from '../data/automat-commits.json'

const max = Math.max(...commits.map(c => c.c))
const BAR = 5, GAP = 1, H = 90
const width = commits.length * (BAR + GAP)
const bars = commits.map((c, i) => ({
  x: i * (BAR + GAP),
  h: Math.max(c.c > 0 ? 1 : 0, Math.round((c.c / max) * H)),
}))
---
<Base title="Projects" description="Things I build and maintain.">
  <p class="section-label">projects</p>

  <div class="project">
    <h2><a href="https://trixbrix.eu">TrixBrix</a><span class="status">active</span></h2>
    <p>
      LEGO-compatible train tracks and accessories. I've been building the company
      and its manufacturing for years: a farm of hundreds of 3D printers, injection molding,
      UV printing, our own electronics, a webshop — everything needed to turn raw
      materials into parcels.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="project">
    <h2>Automat<span class="status">active</span></h2>
    <p>
      The internal software that runs TrixBrix. It started in December 2017 with one
      question: how do you plan printing when orders are made of products, products
      are made of parts, and every printer prints one part at a time? Then it grew
      into the company's operating system. One Rails app, ~70k lines of Ruby,
      3,913 commits — nearly all of them mine. Today it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>plans each night's production across hundreds of printers and four shifts, and can trace every printed part back to the order that justified it</li>
      <li>gives the floor two apps of its own: Pip-Boy, where operators drive printers by scanning QR stickers with a phone, and Assembly Line, where assemblers record builds and stock updates itself</li>
      <li>owns the catalogue — parts, products, bills of materials, sliced plates, filaments, colour variants — and the warehouse, with shelving recommendations and continuous stocktaking</li>
      <li>controls the hall's electricity: idle printers get powered down, the farm powers up in a deliberate sequence, the air conditioning goes last</li>
      <li>sells through the webshop, eBay, Amazon, eMAG, Kaufland and Etsy, all from one catalogue</li>
      <li>audits courier invoices parcel by parcel — it has caught real billing errors and quiet price increases</li>
      <li>prices everything from print telemetry and measures the outcome of every price change before trusting it</li>
      <li>does the books: bank imports, auto-categorisation, an AI second opinion on the yearly report</li>
      <li>keeps the complete tax and customs paper trail and hands the accountant a ready-made monthly package</li>
      <li>handles HR, from leave requests to OCR-ing paper timesheets</li>
      <li>watches the fleet's health: print reliability, error patterns, per-product margins</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="chart">
      <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${width} ${H}`} role="img" aria-label="Monthly commits to Automat since 2017">
        {bars.map(b => (
          <rect class="bar" x={b.x} y={H - b.h} width={BAR} height={b.h} />
        ))}
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="chart-caption">
      3,913 commits, monthly, 2017 → today. Guess when AI agents showed up.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="project">
    <h2><a href="https://github.com/ncr/omarchy-headphones">Omaphones</a><span class="status">active</span></h2>
    <p>
      Headphone controls in the Omarchy bar: per-earbud battery over Google Fast Pair,
      noise-cancelling modes over protocols I had to reverse engineer first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="project">
    <h2><a href="https://github.com/ncr/rack-proxy">rack-proxy</a><span class="status">old, maintained</span></h2>
    <p>
      A small Ruby library I wrote years ago that accidentally became plumbing for
      much bigger projects. By far my most downloaded code:
      <a href="https://rubygems.org/gems/rack-proxy">176 million downloads</a> on RubyGems.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label">electronics</p>
  <div class="project">
    <ul>
      <li>an automatic SD card changer for 3D printers</li>
      <li>an old-school game console with an ESP32 as USB host, so it takes ordinary USB game controllers</li>
      <li>the electronics inside TrixBrix products</li>
      <li>a machine that places pixels</li>
      <li>a rocking cradle that rinses ABS-clogged printer nozzles in acetone</li>
      <li>a brick color sorter with a triple color sensor — it tells brick colors apart much better than I can</li>
      <li>a smartwatch that does exactly one thing: show telemetry from my Onewheel</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This list keeps growing.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
